\begin_layout Title
Chapter 3: Consumer Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Need to complete: Corollary 8, Proposition 34, Proposition 36
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Welfare evaluation of price changes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Up to this point, we have studied the preference-based theory of consumer
 demand from a positive (behavioral) perspective.
 In this section, we present the normative side of consumer theory, called
 
\emph on
welfare analysis
\emph default
.
 Welfare analysis concerns itself with the evaluaiton of the effects of
 changes in the consumer's environment on her well-being.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We investigate the ranking of price-wealth combinations by an agent who
 is a preference maximizer.
 We assume that we only know the agent's choices in the form of a demand
 function.
 In principle, we could reconstruct a utility function that generates the
 agent's demand (this is implicit in the proof of the Integrability Theorem).
 We follow an alternative approach that turns out to be simpler.
 We concentrate on the ranking of the particular situations of interest,
 i.e., the ranking of the agent's choices at two price-wealth combinations,
 say 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the sake of simplicity in the presentation, we focus on the welfare
 effect of a price change, so we analyze the ranking of price-wealth combination
s that differ only in their price vector.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We do not consider changes that affect wealth here.
 However, the analysis readily extends to that case.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We assume that the consumer has a fixed wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 and the price vector is initially 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to evaluate the impact on the consumer's welfare of a change from
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 to a new price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Although any indirect utility function derived from preferences suffices
 for making comparisons between 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

, one class of indirect utility functions deserves special mention because
 it leads to measurement of the welfare change expressed in dollar units.
 These are called 
\emph on
money metric
\emph default
 indirect utility functions and are constructed by means of the expenditure
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(85).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated continuous preference.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
money metric representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the function, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}$
\end_inset

, defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
m_{p}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & x & \mapsto & m_{p}(x)\equiv{\displaystyle \min_{y\geq0,y\succsim x}}\ p\cdot y
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(30).

\series default
 
\emph default
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference relation.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the money metric representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The proof of this proposition is given by Problem 11, Section 3.3.3 and Problem
 2, Section 3.5.9.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
Here, and in the rest of this section, it would suffice to assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated, continuous, and such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim0$
\end_inset

.
 We also assume, whenever convenient, that the consumer's expenditure and
 indirect utility functions are differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A money metric indirect utility function can be constructed in the manner
 presented by Definition 85 for any price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Two particularly natural choices for the price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}$
\end_inset

 are the initial price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and the new price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 These choices lead to two well-known measures of welfare change, the 
\emph on
equivalent variation
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $(EV)$
\end_inset

 and the 
\emph on
compensating variation 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $(CV)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(86).

\series default
 
\emph on
The 
\series bold
equivalent variation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 at wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
EV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w) & \equiv & m_{p}(x(p^{\prime},w))-m_{p}(x(p,w))\\
 & = & e(p,x(p^{\prime},w))-e(p,x(p,w))\\
 & = & e(p,x(p^{\prime},w))-w
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The equivalent variation uses 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 as the reference point for the money metric representation.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $EV$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as the dollar amount that the consumer would be indifferent
 about accepting in lieu of the price change; that is, it is the change
 in her wealth that would be 
\emph on
equivalent
\emph default
 to the price change in terms of its welfare impact (so it is negative if
 the price change would make the consumer worse off).
 In particular, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x(p^{\prime},w))$
\end_inset

 is the wealth level at which the consumer achieves indifference to 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

, the level generated by the price change, at prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x(p^{\prime},w))-w$
\end_inset

 is the net change in wealth that causes the consumer to be indifferent
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

 at prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 We can also express the equivalent variation using the indirect utility
 funciton in the following way: 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w+EV)=u(x(p^{\prime},w))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(87).

\series default
\emph on
 The 
\series bold
compensating variation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 at wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
CV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w) & \equiv & m_{p^{\prime}}(x(p^{\prime},w))-m_{p^{\prime}}(x(p,w))\\
 & = & e(p^{\prime},x(p^{\prime},w))-e(p^{\prime},x(p,w))\\
 & = & w-e(p^{\prime},x(p,w))
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The compensating variation uses 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 as the reference point for the money metric representation.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $CV$
\end_inset

 measures the net revenue of a planner who must 
\emph on
compensate
\emph default
 the consumer for the price change after it occurs, making her indifferent
 to her original bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 (Hence, the compensating variation is negative if the planner would have
 to pay the consumer a positive level of compensation because the price
 change makers her worse off.) It can be thought of as the negative of the
 amount that the consumer would be just willing to accept from the planner
 to allow the price change to happen.
 The compensating variation can be expressed in the following way: 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p^{\prime},w-CV)=u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(31).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated demand correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the associated demand function.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\gg0$
\end_inset

, the following three statements are equivalent:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)\succsim x(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $EV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w)\geq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $CV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w)\geq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
The proof of this proposition is given by Problem 3, Section 3.5.9.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because both the 
\begin_inset Formula $EV$
\end_inset

 and the 
\begin_inset Formula $CV$
\end_inset

 correspond to measurements of the changes in a money metric indirect utility
 function, both provide a correct welfare ranking of the alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

; that is, the consumer is better off under 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 if and only if these measures are positive.
 In general however, the specific dollar amounts calculated using the EV
 and CV measures will differ because of the differing price vectors at which
 compensation is assumed to occur in these two measures of welfare change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The equivalent and compensating variations have interesting representations
 in terms of the Hicksian demand curve.
 Suppose, for simplicity, that only the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 changes, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}\neq p_{l}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{-l}=p_{-l}^{\prime}=\bar{p}_{-l}$
\end_inset

.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $(p_{-l},t)$
\end_inset

 be the vector obtained by replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(32).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated choice correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w)=\mbox{sign}(p_{l}-t){\displaystyle \int_{\min\{t,p_{l}\}}^{\max\{t,p_{l}\}}}h_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),x((p_{-l},t),w))\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\shape up
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated choice correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
EV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w) & = & m_{p}(x((p_{-l},t),w))-m_{p}(x(p,w))\\
 & = & m_{p}(x((p_{-l},t),w))-w\\
 & = & e(p,x((p_{-l},t),w))-e((p_{-l},t),x((p_{-l},t),w))\\
 & = & e((p_{-l},p_{l}),x((p_{-l},t),w))-e((p_{-l},t),x((p_{-l},t),w))
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

 be the function defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
\varphi: & \mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & \tau & \mapsto & \varphi(\tau)\equiv e((p_{-l},\tau),x((p_{-l},t),w))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varphi(p_{l})-\varphi(t)=\mbox{sign}(p_{l}-t){\displaystyle \int_{\min\{t,p_{l}\}}^{\max\{t,p_{l}\}}}\frac{d\varphi(\tau)}{d\tau}\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset

Since for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{d\varphi(\tau)}{d\tau}=\frac{\partial e((p_{-l},\tau),x((p_{-l},t),w))}{\partial p_{l}}=h_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),x((p_{-l},t),w))
\]

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w)=\mbox{sign}(p_{l}-t){\displaystyle \int_{\min\{t,p_{l}\}}^{\max\{t,p_{l}\}}}h_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),x((p_{-l},t),w))\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the change in consumer welfare as measured by the equivalent variation
 can be represented by the area lying between 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and to the left of the Hicksian demand curve for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 associated with the utility level of 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

 (it is equal to this area if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}<p_{l}$
\end_inset

 and is equal to its negative if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}>p_{l}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(8).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated choice correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\gg0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $EV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

 is equal to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w)={\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}}\mbox{sign}(p_{l}-p_{l}^{\prime})\Phi(l)
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Phi(l)\equiv{\displaystyle \intop_{\min\{p_{l},p_{l}^{\prime}\}}^{\max\{p_{l},p_{l}^{\prime}\}}}h_{l}\left((p_{-\{1,\ldots,l\}},p_{\{1,\ldots,l-1\}}^{\prime},\tau),x((p_{-\{1,\ldots,l\},}p_{\{1,\ldots,l\}}^{\prime}),w)\right)\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Similarly, the compensating variation can be written in a similar manner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(33).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated choice correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w)={\displaystyle \int_{t}^{p_{l}}}h_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),x(p,w))\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\shape up
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated choice correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
CV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w) & = & m_{(p_{-l},t)}(x((p_{-l},t),w))-m_{(p_{-l},t)}(x(p,w))\\
 & = & w-m_{(p_{-l},t)}(x(p,w))\\
 & = & e(p,x(p,w))-e((p_{-l},t),x(p,w))\\
 & = & e((p_{-l},p_{l}),x(p,w))-e((p_{-l},t),x(p,w))
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

 be the function defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
\varphi: & \mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & \tau & \mapsto & \varphi(\tau)\equiv e((p_{-l},\tau),x(p,w))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varphi(p_{l})-\varphi(t)={\displaystyle \int_{t}^{p_{l}}}\frac{d\varphi(\tau)}{d\tau}\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset

Since for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{d\varphi(\tau)}{d\tau}=\frac{\partial e((p_{-l},\tau),x(p,w))}{\partial p_{l}}=h_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),x(p,w))
\]

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w)={\displaystyle \int_{t}^{p_{l}}}h_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),x(p,w))\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that we now use the initial utility level of 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is a normal good, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $EV(p,p^{\prime},w)>CV(p,p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

.
 This relation between the 
\begin_inset Formula $EV$
\end_inset

and that 
\begin_inset Formula $CV$
\end_inset

 reverses when good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is inferior.
 However, if there is no wealth effect for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 (e.g., if the underlying preferences are quasilinear with respect to some
 good 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq l$
\end_inset

), the 
\begin_inset Formula $CV$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $EV$
\end_inset

 measures are the 
\emph on
same
\emph default
 because then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h_{l}(p_{l},\bar{p}_{-l},x(p,w))=x_{l}(p_{l},\bar{p}_{-l},w)=h_{l}(p_{l},\bar{p}_{-l},x(p^{\prime},w))
\]

\end_inset

In this case of no wealth effects, we call the common value of 
\begin_inset Formula $CV$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $EV$
\end_inset

, which is also the value of the area lying between 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and to the left of the market (i.e., Walrasian) demand curve for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, the change in 
\emph on
Marshallian consumer surplus
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(34).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 that is quasi-linear with respect to commodtiy 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and whose associated demand correspondence is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the associated demand function.
 
\begin_inset Formula $Let$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $l\neq k$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w)=CV^{\succsim}(p,(p_{-l},t),w)={\displaystyle \int_{t}^{p_{l}}}x_{l}((p_{-l},\tau),w)\ d\tau
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
See Problem 7, Section 3.5.7.
 Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.6.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Welfare evaluation of price-wealth changes in a partial equilibrium environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 that is quasi-linear with respect to commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and whose associated demand correspondence is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the associated demand function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(88).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $l\neq k$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
Marshallian demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the function, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}^{M}$
\end_inset

, defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
x_{l}^{M}: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & p & \mapsto & x_{l}^{M}(p)\equiv x_{l}(p,w)\ \mbox{for some}\ w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


